Templates (Jinja2)

Today I learned about Jinja2 Templates in Ansible, which are used to create dynamic configuration files instead of static ones 🧩
Templates allow us to generate configs based on variables, conditions, and loops.
 This makes automation flexible and environment-aware ⚡

🔹 🧠 Jinja2 Basics
Jinja2 is a templating engine used in Ansible to insert dynamic values into files.

It uses:
 {{ }} → for variables
 {% %} → for logic (if, for, etc.)
This helps in generating customized configuration files automatically.

🔹 📦 Variables in Templates
We can directly use Ansible variables inside templates using Jinja2 syntax.

Example:
server_name = {{ app_name }}
port = {{ app_port }}
Here, values are dynamically replaced during Playbook execution.
 No need to manually edit config files ✨

🔹 🔁 if / for in Templates
Jinja2 supports conditional and loop logic inside templates.
✔ if condition → For decision-based configs
 ✔ for loop → For multiple entries generation

Example logic:
{% if env == "production" %}
debug = false
{% endif %}
This allows one template to work for multiple environments 🌍

🔹 ⚙️ Config File Generation
Templates are mainly used with the template module to generate configuration files.

Example use case:
 • Generate Nginx config
 • Create app config files
 • Dynamic environment-based settings
Templates ensure consistent and automated configuration management 📄

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Jinja2 enables dynamic configuration files.
 Templates use variables, loops, and conditions.
 One template can serve multiple environments.
